WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.106
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.406 --> 00:00:09.118
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:16.819
Unit twenty-nine: Review: Reading Critically. Today you will ask precise academic questions to evaluate texts.

4
00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:19.899
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:24.287
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.360
Critical reading.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.486
Critical reading means reading that questions and evaluates a text.

8
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.710
Critical reading goes beyond understanding.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:37.865
Your turn. Say: Critical reading.

10
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.073
Critical reading.

11
00:00:42.817 --> 00:00:43.806
Assumption.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:48.140
Assumption means something accepted as true without proof.

13
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.121
The article rests on a hidden assumption.

14
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.613
Your turn. Say: Assumption.

15
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:58.503
Assumption.

16
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.286
Evidence.

17
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:04.130
Evidence means information that supports a claim.

18
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:06.739
The author provides little evidence.

19
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.979
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

20
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:13.811
Evidence.

21
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.679
Perspective.

22
00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:19.217
Perspective means a particular point of view.

23
00:01:19.817 --> 00:01:22.281
The report ignores the farmers' perspective.

24
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:25.605
Your turn. Say: Perspective.

25
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:29.607
Perspective.

26
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.212
Bias.

27
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:35.041
Bias means an unfair preference for one side.

28
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.824
Check the source for possible bias.

29
00:01:38.417 --> 00:01:40.930
Your turn. Say: Bias.

30
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:44.837
Bias.

31
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:46.573
Context.

32
00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:51.301
Context means the circumstances in which a text was produced.

33
00:01:51.917 --> 00:01:54.265
Historical context changes the meaning.

34
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.398
Your turn. Say: Context.

35
00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:01.409
Context.

36
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.000
Evaluate.

37
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:07.409
Evaluate means to judge the quality or value of something.

38
00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:10.190
Evaluate the strength of each argument.

39
00:02:10.783 --> 00:02:13.296
Your turn. Say: Evaluate.

40
00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:17.262
Evaluate.

41
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:19.238
Interpretation.

42
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.004
Interpretation means an explanation of meaning.

43
00:02:23.617 --> 00:02:26.417
Offer an interpretation supported by evidence.

44
00:02:27.017 --> 00:02:29.704
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

45
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:33.812
Interpretation.

46
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.344
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:39.183 --> 00:02:44.796
Visal, this is our final unit. What is the most important question a critical reader asks?

48
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:54.526
To what extent does the author support this claim? That single question forces me to check the evidence instead of trusting the tone.

49
00:02:55.617 --> 00:02:56.769
And after that?

50
00:02:57.883 --> 00:03:06.863
What assumptions does the writer make, and whose perspective is missing? Those questions reveal bias that isn't obvious at first.

51
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.170
How has this course changed your reading?

52
00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:21.680
I no longer ask only what a text says. I ask how it creates meaning, in what context it was written, and whom it is trying to persuade.

53
00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:24.485
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:29.045
Grammar focus: Question forms in academic inquiry.

55
00:03:29.345 --> 00:03:30.624
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.317 --> 00:03:33.815
To what extent does the author support this claim?

57
00:03:34.717 --> 00:03:36.774
What assumptions does the writer make?

58
00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:39.973
In what context was the text written?

59
00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:53.640
Academic questions are open and evaluative: 'To what extent…?', 'How far…?', 'In what ways…?'. Remember question order (auxiliary + subject + verb) unless the question word is the subject: 'Whose perspective is missing?'

60
00:03:54.383 --> 00:03:59.137
Be careful! Don't say: To what extent the author supports this claim?

61
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.237
Say: To what extent does the author support this claim?

62
00:04:04.537 --> 00:04:07.280
Use question word order: does + subject.

63
00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:10.928
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:13.628
Becoming a Critical Reader.

65
00:04:13.928 --> 00:04:19.144
Throughout this course, we have moved from asking what texts mean to asking how and why they mean it.

66
00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:26.997
A critical reader approaches every text, whether a poem, an advert or a news report, with structured questions.

67
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.856
To what extent are the claims supported by evidence, and what assumptions lie beneath them?

68
00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:39.013
Whose perspective is represented, and whose has been marginalised or left out entirely?

69
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.272
Such questions do not destroy our enjoyment of reading; rather, they make us more independent and thoughtful citizens.

70
00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:51.415
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.923
To what extent blank the evidence support the claim?

72
00:04:55.223 --> 00:04:58.168
A, do. B, does. C, is.

73
00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:03.547
The answer is B: does.

74
00:05:04.683 --> 00:05:06.311
Choose the correct question.

75
00:05:06.611 --> 00:05:13.237
A, Whose perspective is missing?. B, Whose perspective does is missing?. C, Whose is perspective missing?.

76
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.252
The answer is A: Whose perspective is missing?.

77
00:05:20.417 --> 00:05:23.519
Something accepted as true without proof is an blank.

78
00:05:23.819 --> 00:05:27.879
A, evidence. B, assumption. C, interpretation.

79
00:05:31.433 --> 00:05:33.340
The answer is B: assumption.

80
00:05:34.483 --> 00:05:36.587
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:36.887 --> 00:05:41.435
A, raise a question. B, rise a question. C, do a question.

82
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.150
The answer is A: raise a question.

83
00:05:48.317 --> 00:05:51.837
According to the reading, critical questions make readers…

84
00:05:52.137 --> 00:05:58.043
A, enjoy reading less. B, more independent and thoughtful. C, trust every text.

85
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.062
The answer is B: more independent and thoughtful.

86
00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:09.569
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:10.417 --> 00:06:12.962
What questions do you ask when reading critically?

88
00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:31.204
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:31.504 --> 00:07:05.801
When I read critically, the first question I ask is to what extent the author supports the main claim with reliable evidence. Then I ask what assumptions lie beneath the argument and whose perspective may be missing. For example, an article praising a new shopping mall might ignore the street vendors who lose their income. Some people feel that such questions make reading less enjoyable. In my experience, though, they make every text more interesting, because I see how it works.

90
00:07:06.101 --> 00:07:07.706
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:08.617 --> 00:07:13.951
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and question forms in academic inquiry.

92
00:07:14.251 --> 00:07:18.842
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

93
00:07:19.142 --> 00:07:25.535
Congratulations! You have finished Online C one, Book two. See you in Online C one, Book three!

94
00:07:25.835 --> 00:07:28.557
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

